Goal: add a --tsv flag to the CLI so tab-separated stdin is parsed with a tab separator, while behaviour without the flag stays exactly as it is now (issue https://github.com/o/csv2json/issues/5).

Current state: bin/csv2json.js reads stdin and calls parse(readFileSync(0, 'utf8')) and ignores argv. src/parse.js exports parse(text, sep = ','). test/csv.test.js uses node:test and already has a 'cli' test that runs bin/csv2json.js via execFileSync. package.json has "test": "node --test".

Steps:
1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether '--tsv' appears anywhere in process.argv.slice(2). If it does, call parse(text, '\t'); if not, call parse(text) the same way it is called today. Keep the output format the same: JSON.stringify(result) followed by one '\n'. Do not change how unknown arguments are handled, and do not add --help, --sep/--delimiter or auto-detection. Do not add quoting rules. Leave src/parse.js alone unless you need to change it, and if you do, don't change how it handles CSV.
2. In test/csv.test.js (or a new test/*.test.js file that node --test picks up), add tests that run bin/csv2json.js with execFileSync('node', ['bin/csv2json.js', '--tsv'], { input, encoding: 'utf8' }) and use assert.equal to check the exact stdout:
   - input 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' -> '[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]\n'
   - input 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' -> '[{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]\n'
   - input 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' -> '[{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]\n'
   Also add a test that runs without the flag on input 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' and expects the output '[{"a\\tb":"1\\t2"}]\n' (a JSON tab escape inside the key and the value). Keep the existing tests.
3. In README.md, add a section on the --tsv flag. Say it reads tab-separated input and give the example invocation `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv`. Keep the existing CSV example.
4. From the repo root, run `npm test` and confirm it exits 0. Then run each of these and compare the output exactly:
   - printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo "exit=$?"  -> [{"a":"1","b":"2"}] then exit=0
   - printf 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv  -> [{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]
   - printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv  -> [{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]
   - printf 'a,b\n1,2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js  -> [{"a":"1","b":"2"}]
   - printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js  -> [{"a\tb":"1\t2"}] (literal backslash-t, the JSON escape)
5. Open one pull request with the CLI change, the tests and the README change.